# Crates built from their published source with a patch of ours applied
# (deploy/rust/patches.sh): name, version, sha256 of the .crate file as
# crates.io serves it. The patch is patches/<name>-<version>.patch.
#
# str0m: an empty data channel message is sent as RFC 8831 6.6 says (one
# zero byte under the "empty" payload identifier); as published it takes a
# stream sequence number and writes no chunk, which stalls an ordered
# channel at the peer. A channel is allocated when its offer is answered,
# not when it is added to one: as published, an offer that is dropped
# (rolled back) leaves its channels' allocations behind, and offering a
# channel with a negotiated id again panics. The channels of an offer are
# allocated in the order they were added, so the first one made has the
# lowest stream id.
# The first error cause of an ABORT chunk the peer sends is kept
# (DirectApi::sctp_abort_cause): as published, the association is reported
# lost without a word of why.
# The m-sections an offer adds take the names the host gives
# (SdpApi::name_mids). As published a new section is named at random, and
# that name is what its packets carry (the MID header extension): a peer
# shown the browser's names ("0", "1", ...) for the sections drops every
# packet of an offer's media as one for a section it does not know.
# ICE credentials have the lengths browsers write (a ufrag of 4 characters,
# a password of 24), at the start, at an ICE restart and in the answer to
# a peer's restart: as published they are 16 and 22, which a description
# shows. The certificate the peer showed in the handshake is kept
# (DirectApi::remote_dtls_certificate): as published only its fingerprint is.
# A closed connection ends its association with an ABORT whose cause is a
# User-Initiated Abort ("Close called"), as a browser's does, sent to DTLS
# before its close_notify (Rtc::close; SCTP's abort is sctp-proto's below):
# as published it starts a graceful SHUTDOWN and closes DTLS at once, so the
# SHUTDOWN is dropped and the peer never hears of the close.
